  "language": "en",
  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "Noise-cancelling headphones do not just block sound. They erase part of it by adding more sound. Sound travels through air as pressure waves with peaks and troughs. A tiny microphone on the outside listens to the noise around you. Inside, the electronics create a copy of that noise. But the copy is flipped. Every peak becomes a trough. The speaker plays this anti-noise at exactly the right moment. When both waves reach your ear, their opposing pressures largely cancel. This is called destructive interference. It works best on steady, low sounds, like an engine's hum. Sudden sounds are harder to predict, so some noise still gets through. So silence is not always less sound. Sometimes it is a carefully timed copy, played in reverse. Thank you.",
  "mean_word_probability": 0.9654538560741155,
